/**
 * ============================================================================
 * PLAYWRIGHT E2E AUTOMATION FRAMEWORK STRUCTURE
 * ============================================================================
 * 
 * This document provides a simple, high-level overview of our End-to-End (E2E) 
 * automation framework architecture. Use this guide to understand how files 
 * interact and to confidently present the design decisions to the team.
 * 
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * CORE ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 
 * Our framework is built using the Page Object Model (POM) design pattern 
 * combined with a Dynamic Configuration Engine.
 * 
 * Instead of hardcoding URLs, selectors, and credentials inside the tests, 
 * the entire system adapts dynamically based on the terminal flags passed 
 * at runtime (such as APP=sales ENV=dev).
 * 
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FILE-BY-FILE BREAKDOWN
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 
 * 1. playwright.config.ts (The Control Center)
 *    - What it does: This is the core settings file for Playwright. It defines 
 *      how tests run, which browsers to open (such as Chromium), timeouts, 
 *      and reporting configurations.
 *    - Why it is smart: It initiates dotenv at the very beginning of the 
 *      lifecycle. This ensures our local configuration variables are securely 
 *      loaded into memory before any test execution begins.
 * 
 * 2. .env (The Vault)
 *    - What it does: Stores sensitive, environment-specific key-value entries 
 *      (such as system passwords and usernames) completely separate from our 
 *      public application logic.
 *    - Why it is smart: Keeps secrets out of source control (Git) while using 
 *      a strict naming pattern (such as APP_ENV_ROLE_USERNAME) to feed the 
 *      dynamic configuration engine.
 * 
 * 3. config/environment.ts (The Dynamic Brain)
 *    - What it does: Acts as the registry map for the entire infrastructure. 
 *      It maps subdomains, URLs, page titles, and DOM elements for both the 
 *      Sales and Pricing applications across all server stages (local, dev, 
 *      staging, prod).
 *    - Why it is smart: Contains the getCredentials() engine. It dynamically 
 *      constructs variable strings based on your terminal input at runtime, 
 *      fetching the correct user credentials out of the .env context without 
 *      manual code updates.
 * 
 * 4. integration/pages/base.page.ts (The Blueprint Parent)
 *    - What it does: The baseline class that every specific page layout 
 *      inherits from. It houses universal browser action abstractions such as 
 *      navigating to routes (navigateTo) or validating window tab headers 
 *      (verifyLoginPageTitle).
 *    - Why it is smart: Reduces boilerplate repetition. If a core framework 
 *      command behavior changes later, we only update it here once rather 
 *      than across dozens of scattered files.
 * 
 * 5. integration/pages/login.page.ts (The Login Component Page Object)
 *    - What it does: Extends BasePage and defines the exact behaviors of our 
 *      authentication screen. It reads selectors dynamically from our 
 *      environment registry and contains reusable user workflows like 
 *      loginAsRole().
 *    - Why it is smart: Separates how to interact with the UI from the test 
 *      logic itself. If the technical UI tags change tomorrow, the test 
 *      scripts stay untouched—you only change the selectors in the config.
 * 
 * 6. logging_with_correct_credentials.spec.ts (The Functional Test Script)
 *    - What it does: The actual executable test suite. It coordinates our 
 *      reusable page components into a clean, human-readable user story loop 
 *      (Open portal, submit user credentials, assert dashboard arrival).
 *    - Why it is smart: Highly readable and entirely declarative. It contains 
 *      no messy element locators or hardcoded credentials, making it robust 
 *      against test flakiness.
 * 
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HOW DATA FLOWS (THE LIFECYCLE)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 
 * When you trigger a test command like:
 * "APP=sales ENV=dev ROLE=admin npx playwright test"
 * 
 * Data flows sequentially across these zones:
 * 
 *   [Terminal Flags] -> passes data to -> [playwright.config.ts]
 *                                                 |
 *                                          (loads .env file)
 *                                                 v
 *   [login.page.ts]  <- resolves keys via <- [environment.ts]
 *          |
 *   (locates fields dynamically)
 *          v
 *   [Browser UI Engine Runs] -> verifies and asserts Dashboard success!
 * 
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * KEY TALKING POINTS FOR PRESENTATIONS
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 
 * When explaining this architecture to stakeholders or developers, emphasize 
 * these three main benefits:
 * 
 * - Maintainability: UI element alterations only require a single-line 
 *   update inside our configuration registries. Test specifications remain 
 *   completely untouched.
 * 
 * - Scalability: Adding an entirely new application module (like a Logistics 
 *   App) simply means appending a new section block to the APP_REGISTRY 
 *   object. The tests automatically support it.
 * 
 * - Security: Hardcoded credentials are completely eliminated from the 
 *   codebase via our segregated environment token mapping strategy.
 * 
 * ============================================================================
 */
